The audit's Phase 6 (RESEARCH-ENGINE-AUDIT-2026-10-08, §K; the execution router of §F):
the Research Engine as an operating system the researcher directs in plain language and
approves step by step.

The planner (packages/engine/src/researchPlanner.ts). researchWorkflow(def, opts) reads the
survey as it is into twelve ordered steps — objective, assumptions, structured hypotheses,
research framework, questionnaire, variables, analysis plan, crosstab and test
recommendations, reporting framework, design document, survey structure, deck — each done,
ready (the engine's own actions, for approval), needs_input (only the researcher can say,
with an example sentence that records it), model (a language model would do it, with the
tier and the prompt the cost preview prices) or blocked (an earlier step first, named).
Pure: it writes nothing and calls nothing. The engine does by itself what needs no prose:
the objective; the population read from the objective and the methodology from its goal,
as assumptions listed; the parsed readings of the hypotheses recorded; a construct per
hypothesis side, named as a noun, with the questions that already measure it (by word
family, or the standard measure's own words); a standard item for an unmeasured construct
the library knows, linked to the construct in the same approval; a mean score for a
multi-item construct; the plan; the test a measured hypothesis lacks (the score's, else the
regression over the items) and a missing priority-1 crosstab; the KPIs from the dependent
constructs; the proposal and the deck; the screener first and the demographics last. The
model is asked only to draft hypotheses when none exist, to structure one the words cannot
be read into, and to write a question for a construct with no standard item. In internal
mode those steps ask the researcher instead — never a silent skip. "start the research
workflow for …", "run the workflow", "what's next?" are read by the engine (a `workflow`
interpretation); standardMeasure exported from nlIntent.

Execution choice. The project setting surveys.settings.ai.mode (internal | cloud), read and
written by POST /api/copilot/workflow (setMode under survey.edit; the sandbox keeps it in the
browser) and switched in the new Workflow tab. Internal: nothing is sent to a language
model — a model turn is refused on its card with its own failure code (internal_mode) and
where the switch is, a sentence with an engine alternative gets the alternative, the
findings outputs skip the narrative. Per request: one request let through to the cloud
("once", used up by the call that takes it) and `mode` on the route for one reading.

Tiers and the cost preview. AI_MODEL_SMALL beside AI_MODEL; aiModelName(tier),
completeJson({ tier }); the meter's estimate and reservation carry the tier (AiEstimate.tier,
aiSpec moved to lib/aiEstimate.ts so it is testable without a server; a fake report under
simulation is still priced at the configured model, a real model name — the tier's — is
kept). The route prices every model step through estimateAi, nothing reserved; the tab
shows the model and the credits next to the button that runs the step and the total of the
cloud steps from here.

The Studio. useCopilot: executionMode/setExecutionMode, cloudOnce/setCloudOnce, workflow
(the planner on the survey as it stands, the open proposal included, so a step applied is
done at once), workflowInfo/refreshWorkflow, showWorkflow, runWorkflowStep (an engine step
is a proposal in Changes recorded in History with workflow: <step>; an output step is the
Phase 5 output and marks the document or the deck produced; a model step is a model turn);
the internal-mode gate before any request leaves. WorkflowTab: the summary, the
Internal/Cloud switch with the configured models, the cost line, the twelve steps with their
marks, reasons, buttons, the examples as chips to the input box, what a blocked step waits
on. The auth-guard audit records the route's setMode as writing the project-configuration
bag, as the config route does.

